1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:02,209
Imagine this.

2
00:00:02,209 --> 00:00:07,422
One quiet evening, a strange message pops up on Mike's computer.

3
00:00:07,422 --> 00:00:10,755
It says: You have thirty days to become a hacker.

4
00:00:10,755 --> 00:00:12,512
What will you do?

5
00:00:12,662 --> 00:00:13,513
Wait.

6
00:00:13,513 --> 00:00:17,912
If I became a hacker for one month, would I be able to open any computer?

7
00:00:18,062 --> 00:00:23,391
Movies often show hackers typing a few commands and instantly gaining access

8
00:00:23,391 --> 00:00:25,114
to the entire world.

9
00:00:25,114 --> 00:00:27,704
Real life is very different.

10
00:00:27,704 --> 00:00:32,945
The word hacker can describe a criminal, or a cybersecurity professional who

11
00:00:32,945 --> 00:00:36,575
looks for weaknesses in order to protect systems.

12
00:00:38,758 --> 00:00:41,541
Mike now has two possible paths.

13
00:00:41,541 --> 00:00:46,721
The first path is illegal: breaking into accounts, stealing information,

14
00:00:46,721 --> 00:00:50,652
reading private messages, or damaging websites.

15
00:00:50,652 --> 00:00:54,040
The second path is ethical cybersecurity.

16
00:00:54,040 --> 00:00:59,225
Here, a professional receives permission, tests a system within agreed limits,

17
00:00:59,225 --> 00:01:02,284
reports problems, and helps fix them.

18
00:01:02,434 --> 00:01:05,701
So the important question isn't only what I can do.

19
00:01:05,701 --> 00:01:07,985
It's whether I have permission to do it.

20
00:01:08,135 --> 00:01:09,265
Exactly.

21
00:01:11,425 --> 00:01:15,348
On day one, Mike writes down three simple rules.

22
00:01:15,348 --> 00:01:17,246
Rule one: get permission.

23
00:01:17,246 --> 00:01:19,380
Rule two: know the limits.

24
00:01:19,380 --> 00:01:21,884
Rule three: report safely.

25
00:01:21,884 --> 00:01:27,104
He will only test systems he owns, or systems where he has clear permission.

26
00:01:27,104 --> 00:01:34,078
That could be his own computer, a training platform, or a security lab built for practice.

27
00:01:34,228 --> 00:01:39,657
I will not test a bank, a stranger's social media account, or my neighbor's computer.

28
00:01:39,807 --> 00:01:44,534
Even if a system looks poorly protected, that does not give anyone the right

29
00:01:44,534 --> 00:01:46,160
to interfere with it.

30
00:01:46,160 --> 00:01:48,652
Permission comes before curiosity.

31
00:01:50,842 --> 00:01:54,392
During the first week, Mike studies the basics.

32
00:01:54,392 --> 00:01:59,745
He learns how websites work, why software updates matter, why multi-factor

33
00:01:59,745 --> 00:02:06,372
authentication is important, and why one password should never be reused everywhere.

34
00:02:06,372 --> 00:02:11,318
He also learns that cybersecurity is not only about computers.

35
00:02:11,318 --> 00:02:17,190
Backups, careful communication, privacy, and employee training matter too.

36
00:02:17,340 --> 00:02:22,001
So sometimes the biggest security problem begins with one careless click?

37
00:02:22,151 --> 00:02:23,163
Yes.

38
00:02:23,163 --> 00:02:28,995
That's why cybersecurity professionals study both technology and human behavior.

39
00:02:31,175 --> 00:02:33,328
So what may Mike do legally?

40
00:02:33,328 --> 00:02:35,244
Quite a lot, actually.

41
00:02:35,244 --> 00:02:41,145
He may review the security of his own computer, his phone, and his home network.

42
00:02:41,145 --> 00:02:45,853
He may practice in training labs, platforms designed specifically for

43
00:02:45,853 --> 00:02:48,041
cybersecurity education.

44
00:02:48,041 --> 00:02:54,286
He may join authorized competitions, where the rules clearly define what is allowed.

45
00:02:54,286 --> 00:02:58,433
And if he notices a possible security problem, he should use the

46
00:02:58,433 --> 00:03:01,474
organization's official reporting channel.

47
00:03:01,474 --> 00:03:07,191
He should never publish private information, or use the problem for personal gain.

48
00:03:09,342 --> 00:03:11,418
He can also help his family.

49
00:03:11,418 --> 00:03:16,880
Mike shows his relatives how to turn on multi-factor authentication, update

50
00:03:16,880 --> 00:03:21,654
their devices, use stronger passwords, and create backups.

51
00:03:21,654 --> 00:03:25,599
Then he hangs a new poster on his wall: Permission first.

52
00:03:25,599 --> 00:03:26,953
Testing second.

53
00:03:29,133 --> 00:03:32,120
But some actions can never be justified.

54
00:03:32,120 --> 00:03:37,371
Not by curiosity, and not by a desire to prove technical skill.

55
00:03:37,371 --> 00:03:43,026
Mike must not guess or steal passwords, enter another person's account, or

56
00:03:43,026 --> 00:03:45,257
read private messages.

57
00:03:45,257 --> 00:03:51,104
He must not install malicious software, shut down websites, or demand money.

58
00:03:51,104 --> 00:03:56,309
He must not publish someone else's private data, or test banks, hospitals,

59
00:03:56,309 --> 00:04:02,346
government systems, or other critical services without explicit authorization.

60
00:04:02,346 --> 00:04:06,296
And he must never use a security weakness for personal gain.

61
00:04:08,467 --> 00:04:11,393
So Mike pushes that temptation away.

62
00:04:11,543 --> 00:04:15,035
Even if I can do something, that doesn't mean I'm allowed to do it.

63
00:04:17,217 --> 00:04:21,503
Near the end of the month, an unexpected email arrives.

64
00:04:21,653 --> 00:04:23,452
We found a security problem.

65
00:04:23,452 --> 00:04:24,971
Prove you're a real hacker.

66
00:04:24,971 --> 00:04:26,602
Send us user data?

67
00:04:26,602 --> 00:04:28,085
This looks suspicious.

68
00:04:28,085 --> 00:04:31,782
I won't send private data, or open unknown files.

69
00:04:31,932 --> 00:04:35,368
Mike verifies the sender through an official channel.

70
00:04:35,368 --> 00:04:40,548
He does not click suspicious links, open unknown attachments, or share

71
00:04:40,548 --> 00:04:42,948
confidential information.

72
00:04:42,948 --> 00:04:48,623
Sometimes, the most important cybersecurity skill is knowing when to stop.

73
00:04:48,623 --> 00:04:52,754
An unknown message is never a reason to take a risk.

74
00:04:54,925 --> 00:04:57,905
Thirty days later, the month is over.

75
00:04:57,905 --> 00:05:03,788
Mike has turned on multi-factor authentication, updated his devices, created

76
00:05:03,788 --> 00:05:11,073
backups, studied the security basics, and even submitted a responsible security report.

77
00:05:11,073 --> 00:05:13,322
He hasn't become a movie hero.

78
00:05:13,322 --> 00:05:17,542
He can't open every computer in the world, and he hasn't learned how to

79
00:05:17,542 --> 00:05:19,756
disappear from the internet.

80
00:05:19,756 --> 00:05:24,290
But he has become more careful, more knowledgeable, and better prepared to

81
00:05:24,290 --> 00:05:27,421
protect himself, and the people he cares about.

82
00:05:29,592 --> 00:05:34,623
A real cybersecurity professional doesn't break into other people's digital homes.

83
00:05:34,623 --> 00:05:38,301
They help find the weak spots before criminals can use them.

84
00:05:38,451 --> 00:05:43,254
If you want to learn cybersecurity, start with digital safety, basic

85
00:05:43,254 --> 00:05:46,187
programming, networking, and privacy.

86
00:05:46,187 --> 00:05:52,921
Practice only on authorized training platforms, and always report problems responsibly.

87
00:05:52,921 --> 00:05:55,240
Because curiosity is useful.

88
00:05:55,240 --> 00:05:57,163
But permission is essential.
